thread:
maria: FYI customer (AcmeRetail) is seeing mid-stream SSE disconnects when users switch from WiFi->cell or when the phone screen sleeps. They report partial outputs (truncated chunks) and the client never reconnects automatically. Escalating to eng, sending logs below.

ajay: Can you paste the request_id and any HTTP status we saw?

maria: request_id=rid_8bf3e2a7, client reports net::ERR_INTERNET_DISCONNECTED in console. Our proxy logs show 200 then connection closed by client after ~42s. No 5xx from our runtime.

elaine: Typical mobile carriers will drop idle connections ~30-60s. Do we have keepalive pings for SSE? Also which client lib are they using?

maria: They use a homegrown EventSource wrapper (vanilla JS) + RN WebView for iOS. Repro steps from customer: 1) Start streaming chat; 2) Toggle WiFi off; 3) stream stops and never resumes; 4) client shows partial last sentence.

sam: Repro'd locally on Android using Chrome: when I flip to airplane mode the socket errors with ECONNRESET and EventSource onerror fires, but their client doesn't implement reconnect logic. EventSource normally retries but WebView wrapper might swallow it.

maria: Attaching server-side excerpt: 
```
[2026-02-12T15:21:10Z] rid=rid_8bf3e2a7 pod=runtime-5c8f recv req /v1/streams 200 OK chunked start
[2026-02-12T15:21:52Z] rid=rid_8bf3e2a7 k8s-lb connection terminated client_reset
[2026-02-12T15:21:52Z] rid=rid_8bf3e2a7 pod=runtime-5c8f stream closed, tokens_sent=432
```

maria: Customer also provided client console: "EventSource failed to connect" then "net::ERR_INTERNET_DISCONNECTED" and they do not supply last-event-id when reconnecting. They see truncated JSON in the UI.

ajay: Two angles: 1) client-side: implement reconnect with last-event-id and exponential backoff + graceful resume; 2) server-side: reduce idle window and send ping comments every 20s to keep mobile NAT/firewalls happy. Also set `Cache-Control: no-transform` and ensure proxy doesn't buffer entire response.

elaine: We should check our edge (cloud LB) idle timeout; GCP/ALBs default 30s. If our runtime doesn't send interim heartbeats on quiet periods > 30s, proxies will close even if runtime still producing later.

sam: Quick repro snippet for customers to test reconnect behavior (vanilla JS):
```
const es = new EventSource('/v1/streams?session=abc');
es.onmessage = (e) => console.log('msg', e.data);
es.onerror = (e) => {
  console.warn('SSE error', e);
  // basic reconnect
  setTimeout(() => {
    // send Last-Event-ID header via fetch then re-open if possible
    const last = es.lastEventId || '';
    const newEs = new EventSource('/v1/streams?last_event_id=' + last);
  }, 1000);
};
```

maria: Customer asked whether Redwood server supports resuming from last-event-id. Thoughts?

ajay: Our runtime supports app-level resume semantics if the model stream includes an event id token offset. We don't automatically persist last-event ids per session. Need engineering to confirm whether the public streaming API accepts `Last-Event-ID` or `last_event_id` query.

elaine: Filing a quick runtime ticket: possible fixes - (A) Emit SSE comment pings every 15s from pipeline when there is >15s silence; (B) Add `Retry:` and guidance on `Last-Event-ID` in docs; (C) Validate LB idle time and add TCP keepalive config on ingress.

sam: For immediate customer workaround: advise client to implement reconnect with last-event-id and show UI that indicates partial output; also suggest they avoid relying on RN WebView EventSource unless they polyfill reconnects.

maria: Customer wants timeline — can eng estimate effort for server ping + docs patch?

ajay: Ping emit is small (runtime patch ~1-2 engineer-days + QA). Docs update + SDK sample ~ half day. Need infra to check LB defaults — tagging infra. @infra-bot can you check LB idle on prod? :pray: 

infra-bot: infra-bot: Checked prod HTTP LB idle: 30s default on region us-west1. TCP keepalive at 60s.

elaine: Okay that matches. 30s is tight. Suggest runtime pings every 15s.

maria: I'll give customer the workaround + ETA for server fix. I'll follow up once we have an internal RFC. Thanks all :thumbsup:

sam: Small note for triage: also watch for 499s in our access logs (client closed request) — that indicates client-side aborts rather than runtime faults.

maria: Adding that: their logs show many 499s correlated with wifi toggles.

ajay: I'll open the runtime PR next sprint to add SSE comment pings and a flag to opt-out for very chatty streams.

maria: Great — please keep me looped. Customer is satisfied with workaround for now.
